# What a module's translation unit holds, decided before a byte of it is
# written and before any probe asks its headers (design.md §4.19; panel 093;
# panel 202's R1, ratified 2026-10-10, defect 559). `emit/unit.hero` writes
# the unit from its plan, and `cli/probing.hero` asks the plan's headers.
#
# **A module's unit reads its own groups' headers and no other module's**,
# the cut every verb makes since panel 202: the headers of its own extern
# functions and constants, of its own group records, and of every group
# record its unit reaches, whose C type the unit spells and so must be able
# to name. Until defect 559 the record-only walk read EVERY group record of
# the program into every unit (`emit/externs.headers`), so a record bound
# beside a `static inline twice` in one module put that header in the other
# module's unit too, where it met the other `twice` (`fprec`), and a macro
# one module's header defines chose another module's record layout
# (`skew`). And the probes read every header of the program in one unit,
# which is the same cause seen from the checks: they now ask each plan's
# own headers. The unit's own groups' headers come first, in the order they
# are written (panel 204's R1), and a record of another module's after them.
#
# **The reach is the unit's own**, the set its four sections are filtered by
# (`unit.hero`), so a record is in a unit's headers exactly when the unit
# spells its type: a fact about the unit in hand, never a premise about which
# module may name which.

use ast
use source
use check/table
use check/state
use ir/containers
use emit/reach
use emit/members
use emit/externs as emit_externs
use emit/decls as emit_decls

# One module's unit, planned: what it defines and declares (`members`), the
# program cut to its members, the types its sections spell, closed as C reads
# them, its own group records, every group record whose header it reads and
# whose fields it asserts, and those headers in the order it includes them.
record Plan
    module: str
    mine: members.Members
    tu_p: containers.Program
    constructed: {i64: bool}
    declared: {i64: bool}
    groups: Groups
    records: {i64: bool}
    headers: [str]
    target: emit_decls.BuildTarget

# A plan per component of the program, in the order the build compiles them
# (`cli/units.components`), from one build's shared tables.
# `target` is the build's: a program's, or `heroes test`'s, whose units define
# their modules' tests and whose root runs them (defect 560).
function plans(components: [str], p: containers.Program, c: state.Checked, s: source.Source, decls: [ast.Decl], whole: members.Whole, target: emit_decls.BuildTarget) -> [Plan]
    out: [Plan] @= []

    for component in components
        out @ out.push(plan(component, p, c, s, decls, whole: whole, target: target))

    return out

function plan(module: str, p: containers.Program, c: state.Checked, s: source.Source, decls: [ast.Decl], whole: members.Whole, target: emit_decls.BuildTarget) -> Plan
    mine = members.members(module, p, c, s, decls, shown: whole.shown, by_decl: whole.by_decl, callees: whole.callees)
    tu_p = members.tu_program(p, keep: mine.defined)

    # THIS TU's reach, and every whole-program section is filtered by
    # it — typedefs, per-type functions, descriptors. Trimming the
    # descriptors alone left `h_parse_Expr_desc` undeclared under
    # satellites that still mentioned it; trimming NOTHING made a TU
    # 19,193 lines where 87% belonged to other modules, and the
    # compiler's own build went 188 s -> 484 s (measured 2026-08-25).
    walked = reach.reach_of(tu_p, c, target: target, shown: members.all_of(tu_p))

    # A PROTOTYPE names types too, and the body walk above cannot see
    # the ones belonging to another TU's function. So the signatures of
    # everything this TU declares join the reach before the filter is
    # built — asked of the value in hand (these exact functions) rather
    # than argued from "an argument must be a local anyway", which is
    # true today and is a premise about the world (CLAUDE.md §11).
    reached: {i64: bool} @= walked.reached

    # A PROBE names types too, and no loop above can see them: an extern
    # is nobody's Heroes function, so it is in neither `defined` nor
    # `needed`. A function-typed parameter (panel 111 R4) is the first
    # boundary type that needs a typedef this filter could withhold, and
    # it arrived as `unknown type name 'h_0fn_294870dd'` at exit 2 on a
    # correct binding. Asked of tu_p, which IS the program the prelude
    # writes its probes from. Parameters only: a probe's own result is
    # always `void` and its call is cast away.
    for f in tu_p.functions
        if emit_externs.extern_spans(decls, f).header.is_err()
            continue

        for slot in f.params
            reached[f.slots[slot].ty] @ true

    for at in mine.needed
        f = p.functions[at]
        reached[f.result] @ true

        for slot in f.params
            reached[f.slots[slot].ty] @ true

    # Every group record this module declares, used or not (defect 416).
    groups = group_records(module, c, s, decls)

    for id in groups.types
        reached[id] @ true

    # The callback table names every function C may call back and its thunk
    # in EVERY TU that can hand C a function (panel 177's item 2), so their
    # signatures' types join the reach too.
    for at in whole.callbacks.functions
        f = p.functions[at]
        reached[f.result] @ true

        for slot in f.params
            reached[f.slots[slot].ty] @ true

    declared = members.declared_reach(c, decls, seed: reach.closed_over(c, reached: reached))
    records: {i64: bool} @= {}

    for decl in groups.decls
        records[decl] @ true

    # ORDER: none, a set; `headers_for` sorts what it walks into written order.
    for id in keys(declared)
        match table.get(c.types, id)
            .named n =>
                if group_record(decls, decl: n.decl)
                    records[n.decl] @ true
            .int_ty | .float_ty | .bool | .str | .ptr | .cstr | .unit    => continue
            .array | .fixed | .map | .fallible | .function_ty | .case_ty => continue
            .failure | .generic | .error                                 => continue

    return Plan(
        module: module,
        mine: mine,
        tu_p: tu_p,
        constructed: walked.constructed,
        declared: declared,
        groups: groups,
        records: records,
        headers: emit_externs.headers_for(tu_p, s, decls, records: ok(records), own: ok(module)),
        target: target
    )

# Whether a declaration is a record of an `extern` group.
function group_record(decls: [ast.Decl], decl: i64) -> bool
    match decls[decl].kind
        .record_decl rec                                             => return !rec.header.is_err()
        .constant_decl | .function_decl | .variant_decl | .test_decl => return false

    return false

# Every group record a module declares, which the module's TU spells whether
# or not a line of its code reaches it (defect 416, 2026-10-06). **A binding
# the header refutes is the program's mistake whether or not a line uses
# it**, and the fused unit, which `--emit-c` and the seed compile (and
# `heroes test` until defect 560), already said so: its per-type functions are asked of every
# declaration. Until that day a module's unit spelled only what its code
# reached, so a handle over a union's tag or a misspelled one that nothing
# used was refused by `test` alone, and `--emit-c` wrote a unit clang refuses
# at exit 0. The layout check already asked every group record with fields
# (`cli/layout.hero`); this reaches the handles, whose only question is their
# spelling.
#
# `decls` are the declarations, which is what the per-type functions are keyed
# by, since a record nothing mentions is in no type the arena holds; `types`
# are what they and their fields are, where the arena holds them, so the
# fields' own functions and typedefs are reached with them.
record Groups
    decls: [i64]
    types: [i64]

function group_records(module: str, c: state.Checked, s: source.Source, decls: [ast.Decl]) -> Groups
    found: [i64] @= []
    typed: [i64] @= []
    index: i64 @= 0

    for decl in decls
        match decl.kind
            .record_decl rec =>
                if !rec.header.is_err() && source.component_at(s, decl.name.start) == module
                    found @ found.push(index)
                    id = table.lookup(c.types, .named(decl: index))

                    if !id.is_err()
                        typed @ typed.push(id.must())

                    for written in members.fields_written(decls, decl: index)
                        field = state.written_type(c, written)

                        if !field.is_err()
                            typed @ typed.push(field.must())
            .constant_decl | .function_decl | .variant_decl | .test_decl => index @ index

        index @ index + 1

    return Groups(decls: found, types: typed)
